### Interest Rate and Cross Currency Swaps

An interest rate swap is a very common bank product. Two counterparties agree on a currency and a notional amount in this currency and on a schedule of payment dates. On these payment dates there will be cash flows between the counterparties. The standard is that one pays a fixed coupon and the other pays a floating coupon based on the LIBOR (London Interbank Offer Rate). This standard interest rate swap is a tool to hedge uncertainties of future interest rates. The fixed rate is normally chosen in such a way that the total value of the swap is zero (or leaves some margin for the bank). There can also be floating payments for both the counterparties, if for example A pays to B quarterly and B to A annually. Relevant for each swap contract is the exact specification of the payment schedules (interest rate fixing dates and value dates), the interest rate and day-count convention, the source of the LIBOR rate, possible fees, exchange of notional amounts. However, this is not a book about interest rate products or the valuation or modeling issues of the very same. The contribution of this book is about how to link such swaps to foreign exchange products. This combination becomes then an FX-linked swap if the FX part of the combination is just an add-on or a true hybrid FX-IR product if the FX and IR (interest rate) part can not be separated. In the following we concentrate on some popular FX-linked swaps.

